		<row valign="top"> 
		  <entry namest="col2" nameend="col9"> 
			 <note id="NOTE_E08A327C533944F49C29F75351F4DA7A"> 
				<ol id="OL_2E5964BA09354A17B1D76AD764146848"> 
				  <li id="LI_6083E2D2E5F7411B8ACFECC60DE99818">SBS - Side-by-side, DIMMs placed is side-by-side, in line placement. 
				  </li> 
				  <li id="LI_32B6A9C9C1B840C9A46B49CB067556B3">B2B - Back-to-Back, DIMMs placed in parallel one to other. 
				  </li> 
				  <li id="LI_151127AA73BD4BCF982D3B3AE5BCF907">SDP: Single Die Package, DDP: Dual Die Package , QDP: Quad Die Package, ODP: Octal Die Package 
				  </li> 
				  <li id="LI_6E97CABEB5C445A59695D1884F1BF737">To avoid excessive latency due to ZQ topology, Intel specifies that each ZQ pin must have an unique ZQ resistor. 
				  </li> 
				  <li id="LI_1DEB89EE65E345B2B29C77EF9328DB56">DDR5 SODIMM maximum speed is 5600. CSODIMM should be used above 5600. 
				  </li> 
				  <li id="LI_09CFFC58452541FCAA67264EDD77C068">DDR5 &gt;6400 requires to define a main clock out of 2 clock differential pairs for each 32-bit channel. DDR5 6400 and above requires to define a main clock out of 2 clock deferential pairs for each 32-bit channel. DDR5 6400 and above CKD Technical Advisory (# 
					 <xref>845985</xref>) that provides guidance how to set the new BIOS policies for DDR5 6400 CLK connectivity that is a different than RVP design. 
				  </li> 
				  <li> DDR5 SODIMM maximum speed is 5600. CSODIMM should be used above 5600. 
				  </li> 
				  <li>For Memory updates, refer to the Nova Lake Platform Memory Enablement Guide (# 
					 <xref href="https://cdrdv2.intel.com/v1/dl/getContent/858687" format="https" scope="external">858687</xref>). 
				  </li> 
				</ol> 
			 </note> 
		  </entry> 
		</row> 
